// Memory Management Demo
//
// In Hemlock, memory is yours to manage. Nothing is garbage-collected behind
// your back, so this example walks through the four things you actually do
// with memory: ask how big a type is, allocate it, use it safely, resize it,
// and hand it back. The golden rule: every alloc/talloc/buffer you make, you
// free exactly once.

// 1. sizeof() - Query type sizes
// You allocate in *bytes*, but you think in *types*. sizeof() bridges the two
// so you never have to hard-code "an i32 is 4 bytes" (which can change across
// platforms for types like ptr).
print(sizeof(u8));  // 1
print(sizeof(i32));  // 4
print(sizeof(f64));  // 8
print(sizeof(ptr));  // 8 (on 64-bit systems)

// 2. talloc() - Typed allocation
// talloc(T, n) reserves room for n values of type T. It's just alloc() with
// the byte math done for you: talloc(i32, 10) is alloc(sizeof(i32) * 10).
let numbers = talloc(i32, 10);  // allocate 10 i32s (40 bytes)
print(numbers);  // prints the raw address - this is a pointer, not the data

// Fresh memory contains garbage (whatever was there before). memset() zeroes
// it so we start from a known state - the same discipline C programmers use.
let elem_size = sizeof(i32);
let total_bytes = elem_size * 10;
memset(numbers, 0, total_bytes);

// 3. buffer() - Safe wrapper with bounds checking
// This is the "safe by choice" half of Hemlock. A raw ptr lets you read/write
// anywhere (fast, dangerous); a buffer remembers its own length and traps
// out-of-bounds access instead of corrupting memory. Reach for buffer when you
// want safety, ptr when you want raw control.
let buf = buffer(256);
buf[0] = 72;  // 'H'
buf[1] = 101;  // 'e'
buf[2] = 108;  // 'l'
buf[3] = 108;  // 'l'
buf[4] = 111;  // 'o'
print(buf[0]);
print(buf.length);  // a buffer knows its size; a raw ptr does not

// 4. realloc() - Resize allocations
// realloc() grows (or shrinks) a block. The returned pointer may differ from
// the old one if the data had to move, so ALWAYS reassign: `p = realloc(p, n)`.
// Using the old pointer after a realloc is a classic use-after-free bug.
let p = alloc(64);
print(p);
p = realloc(p, 128);
print(p);
p = realloc(p, 256);
print(p);

// Clean up
// Each allocation above is freed exactly once. Forgetting one is a leak;
// freeing one twice is a double-free crash. Hemlock won't stop you from either
// - that visibility is the point.
free(numbers);
free(buf);
free(p);
